<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Tuvan People's Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1921 CE – 1944 CE · capital Kyzyl</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A nominally independent Soviet satellite in southern Siberia, recognised only by the USSR and Mongolia, which was quietly annexed in 1944.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After years of civil war on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yenisei-river">Yenisei</a>, Tuvan leaders with Soviet support proclaimed the People's Republic of Tannu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuva">Tuva</a> in August 1921, replacing the Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uryankhay-krai">Uryankhay Krai</a>. Its capital, renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyzyl">Kyzyl</a>, lay near the geographical centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyan-badyrgy">Buyan-Badyrgy</a> in the 1920s the state followed a moderate, Buddhist-friendly course. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> had him removed and later executed, and from 1932 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salchak-toka">Salchak Toka</a>, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuvan-peoples-revolutionary-party">Tuvan People's Revolutionary Party</a>, ran a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stalinism">Stalinist</a> regime: herds were collectivised, monasteries closed and lamas persecuted. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet for Tuvan was introduced in 1930.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuva">Tuva</a> gave horses, gold and volunteers to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. In October 1944 it was admitted to the USSR as an autonomous oblast of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salchak-toka">Salchak Toka</a> remained its party leader until 1973.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tuvan-peoples-republic.jpg" alt="Tuvan People's Republic" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Lumia1234 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Tuvan_People%27s_Republic_(1935-1939).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1921 CE – 1944 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kyzyl</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Buyan-Badyrgy, Salchak Toka</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tibetan Buddhism, Tuvan shamanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tuvan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Russia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tannu Tuva, People's Republic of Tannu Tuva</dd></dl></aside></div>
